# Protected Routes

Use Expo Router `*.Protected` for authentication and authorization flows.

- Prefer Protected Routes over manual redirects
- Keep auth logic centralized
- Prefer route groups for authenticated areas
- Avoid duplicating screens across protected groups
- Remove inaccessible screens from navigation

## Stack Example

```tsx
import { Stack } from "expo-router";

export default function Layout() {
  const isAuthenticated = true;

  return (
    <Stack>
      <Stack.Protected guard={!isAuthenticated}>
        <Stack.Screen name="login" />
      </Stack.Protected>

      <Stack.Protected guard={isAuthenticated}>
        <Stack.Screen name="(app)" />
      </Stack.Protected>
    </Stack>
  );
}
```

## Route Structure

```txt
app/
  _layout.tsx

  login.tsx

  (app)/
    _layout.tsx
    index.tsx
    profile.tsx
```

## Nested Protection

Use nested guards for roles and permissions.

```tsx
<Stack.Protected guard={isAuthenticated}>
  <Stack.Protected guard={isAdmin}>
    <Stack.Screen name="admin" />
  </Stack.Protected>

  <Stack.Screen name="profile" />
</Stack.Protected>
```

## Tabs Example

```tsx
import { Tabs } from "expo-router";

export default function Layout() {
  const isAuthenticated = true;

  return (
    <Tabs>
      <Tabs.Screen name="index" />

      <Tabs.Protected guard={isAuthenticated}>
        <Tabs.Screen name="profile" />
      </Tabs.Protected>

      <Tabs.Protected guard={!isAuthenticated}>
        <Tabs.Screen name="login" />
      </Tabs.Protected>
    </Tabs>
  );
}
```

## Best Practices

- Prefer a single auth source of truth
- Prefer context or store for auth state
- Keep guards simple and predictable
- Prefer automatic fallback navigation
- Avoid manual navigation guards when possible
- Protected routes are client-side only
- Do not rely on protected routes for backend security
